<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 45 M3, AC3.3 / D4b: the LINEAR REFERENCE. With a clean master the
  ring BYPASSES the master lane and reads 0.082071 - half what
  master_closure_heard_ring reads over the identical fixture. That pair is the
  mode itself, and it is why this case has no master insert at all.

  THIS CASE IS THE REFERENCE HALF OF A PAIR AND DOES NOT BITE ON ITS OWN, by
  construction rather than by omission. A sabotage that makes a CLEAN master
  report Closure leaves this level untouched, because a Closure whose master
  node has no inserts and unity gain is a no-op - which is precisely why the
  linear split is legitimate in the first place. The discriminating half is
  master_closure_heard_ring, watched failing under two independent sabotages
  (suppressing the frozen PULL instead of the SUM, and refusing every
  non-linear master).

  WHAT THIS CASE IS NOT: a latch check. A version of it that inserted a 2.0x
  master, armed, disarmed, removed the insert and then measured was written and
  ABANDONED, for two measured reasons worth recording rather than rediscovering.
  First, the playback capture is CUMULATIVE within one process, so the arm that
  enters the closure puts its own monitored audio in front of the window and
  the measurement is contaminated (0.105867 where the clean run reads 0.082071).
  Second, a master insert made while a lane is ALREADY monitoring does not
  rebuild the live plan at all, so the closure is never entered and a sabotage
  that LATCHES twSpeaker's closure mode passes the case. Both are stated in the
  plan as ungated; neither is fixed here.

  ONE MEASUREMENT PER CASE, AND THAT IS NOT STYLE. The playback capture is
  CUMULATIVE across phases in one process - arming, playing, stopping and
  disarming does not restart the device, so the recording grows (measured:
  256000, 535552, 815104, 1094656 frames over four phases) and a fixed window
  reads the FIRST phase every time. A four-phase version of this gate returned
  the identical 0.100520 four times over and would have passed while measuring
  nothing. The four cases below therefore share a fixture and a window and
  differ in one setting each.

  THE FOUR RELATED CASES, whose bands are DISJOINT by construction - that is
  what makes them a discriminator rather than four sanity checks:

    master_closure_linear_ring            ring alone, no master   0.082071
    master_closure_linear_ring             ring alone, 2.0x master 0.164142
    master_closure_heard_root             + root at -12 dB        0.201010
    master_closure_heard_root_raised      + root at  -6 dB        0.283790

  Rows 1 vs 2 are the mode itself: under the LINEAR split the ring bypasses the
  master, under CLOSURE it goes through it. Same fixture, same window, one
  master insert apart. Rows 3 vs 4 are the ROOT-PRESENCE
  discriminator - raising the unarmed track 6 dB must move the level by
  1.4118. Before AC3.1 was rebuilt to suppress the frozen SUM rather than the
  frozen PULL, the same change moved it by 1.0046: the arrangement was absent,
  because twSpeaker publishes the transport position from
  engine->currentPosition() AFTER the pull it had stopped making, so the
  playhead never advanced.

  THE MEASUREMENT WINDOW IS THE HARD PART, and a first attempt got it wrong in
  both directions. TOO EARLY sits inside the monitor priming lag (twSpeaker
  defers the device start until the readahead is primed) and read BIMODALLY -
  0.23158 four times and 0.461913 once, same binary, same script. TOO LATE
  runs past the end of the material, halves one source, and reads exactly like
  the other source alone; that artifact was very nearly written up as an
  engine defect. Hence: the fixture is placed TWICE for 8 s and the window is
  2.861 s after the onset, which on this box is [168000, 216000) - 3.5 to
  4.5 s.

  ...AND THE PRIMING LAG CAME BACK ANYWAY (QBX-228). Moving the window past
  the lag did not remove the lag; it made it smaller than the band ON THIS BOX
  and not on CI. The window was ABSOLUTE, and captured frame 0 is the DEVICE
  START rather than the locator position, so the leading silence in front of
  it is a wall-clock quantity of the machine - which is precisely what
  sdumpplaybackcaptureaction.h says at length, ending "Anything measured over
  this file must do the same: bound the start with assert-audio-onset, and
  begin the measurement window one device block in." This case did not, and
  that is the whole defect.

  THE BAND WAS A POSITIONAL TOLERANCE IN DISGUISE. Over RAMPING material a
  displaced window reads as an amplitude difference, and the sweep says how
  much: the +/- 3 % band admits -2028..+2008 frames of head slip, so CI's
  2044-frame displacement missed it by SIXTEEN FRAMES' worth. Widening the
  band was therefore exactly the wrong remedy - at 3 % it already tolerates
  two capture blocks, and widening it to admit 2044 would have admitted any
  displacement at all up to ~2050, retiring most of what the window is
  positioned to detect.

  WHAT THE CI FAILURES ACTUALLY WERE. The failing value was BIT-IDENTICAL on
  two different commits a day apart - 0.0845199 on PR #296 (a docs-only
  change) and again on PR #302 - while a re-run of the SAME commit passed.
  Two exactly reproducible outcomes is a DISCRETE state difference, not
  jitter, which rules out a numerical difference between the runners' builds
  (that could not pass on a re-run) and any fixed slip. The frame counts were
  identical both sides, 256000, because the dump length is fixed and the HEAD
  moves inside it - which is why QBX-222's frame-count triage misses this one.
  The window is now anchored on the onset and the displacement is injected
  below, so the failure reproduces locally and deterministically instead of
  waiting for a runner to show it.

  AND IT IS NOT CI-ONLY, which is where the ticket's own premise was wrong.
  While fixing this, master_closure_heard_ring switched modes BETWEEN ctest
  rounds on the dev box: its absolute window went from 0.164143 to 0.169044 -
  exactly the -2048-frame value the sweep predicts - and then read 0.169044
  four runs running, bit-stable in the new mode. Its ceiling is 0.1691, so it
  passed by 0.00006. So the displacement happens here too; CI is not special,
  it just lands in the mode that misses the band. "Bit-stable locally" was
  true of each MODE and said nothing about which mode a run would get.

  THE ANCHOR WAS THEN VALIDATED ON THAT REAL SWITCH, not only on the injected
  one: over the displaced capture the absolute window reads 0.169044 (+2.98 %)
  while the anchored window reads 0.164666 (+0.32 %), a ninefold reduction.
  The residual is the anchor's own under-correction - the 0.02 crossing moved
  1826 frames where the material moved about 2048 - and 0.32 % sits well
  inside a 3 % band.

  WHY THE DEFAULT 0.02 THRESHOLD AND NOT A LOWER ONE. A lower threshold looks
  better in principle: it should land nearer the device start, where the
  landmark is a timing quantity rather than a level crossing on this ramping
  material. Measured, it is worse. At 0.0005 the two modes put heard_ring's
  onset at 4097 and 2196 - moving the OPPOSITE way to the material, because
  the displaced head carries quiet junk that crosses a low line early while
  the real material arrives late. The 0.02 crossing moved +1826, i.e. WITH the
  material, which is the only direction that corrects anything. The house
  default is also what assert-metronome-clicks anchors its grid on, so the
  two surfaces agree on where an onset is.

  THE SOURCES ARE UNCORRELATED ON PURPOSE: the root carries test_autosaw.wav
  and the ring test_sawtooth.wav, so their sum is sqrt of the sum of squares
  regardless of a relative phase nothing here controls. Two copies of one
  signal have no closed-form sum, and every band would have to widen until it
  separated nothing.

  HEADROOM IS PART OF THE MEASUREMENT, NOT HOUSEKEEPING. The armed track runs
  through a 0.25 gain so the 2.0x master does not clip. An earlier probe put
  that master over full-scale sources, clipped the 16-bit capture at peak
  exactly 1.0000, and read as an unexplained ~11 % shortfall.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="master_closure_linear_ring">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- Track 0: armed and monitored, on the paced file input carrying
         test_sawtooth.wav (RMS 0.328284), through a 0.25 gain = 0.082071. -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <set-track-input trackPath="0" input="audio:file:3"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="0" mode="on"/>
    <insert-plugin trackPath="0" slotIndex="0"
                   format="clap" uid="tw.test.clap.gain"
                   name="Smaragd Test Gain" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="twtestclap.clap" nIn="2" nOut="2"/>
    <set-plugin-param trackPath="0" slotIndex="0" paramId="0" value="0.25"/>

    <!-- Track 1: NEVER armed. test_autosaw.wav (RMS 0.230956) placed twice for
         8 s of material. -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="1" filePath="../test_autosaw.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="1" filePath="../test_autosaw.wav" timePos="192000"/>
    <set-track-volume trackPath="1" volume="-60"/>
    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="1"/>
    <set-locator position="0"/>
    <toggle-playback play="1"/>
    <wait-playhead minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="30000"/>
    <wait-ms ms="5200"/>
    <toggle-playback play="0"/>
    <dump-playback-capture filename="master_closure_linear_ring.wav" minFrames="216000"/>

    <!-- BOUND AND REPORT THE ONSET, which is the house rule
         sdumpplaybackcaptureaction.h states for anything measured over this
         file: "bound the start with assert-audio-onset, and begin the
         measurement window one device block in". The bound is also what keeps
         the anchored window INSIDE the capture: onset + 185320 must stay
         under the 256000 frames captured, so an onset past 65536 would make
         the analyser read a short region and the band would mean something
         else. assert-audio-onset reports the number whether the bounds pass
         or fail, so a future red run names the quantity that moved. -->
    <assert-audio-onset filename="master_closure_linear_ring.wav" channel="0"
                        maxFrame="40000"/>

    <!-- ANCHORED ON THE ONSET, not on frame 0 (QBX-228). 137320 = the old
         absolute 168000 minus this box's onset of 30680, so the window covers
         the same 2.861 s-after-audio-starts it always did. What changed is
         that it now follows the capture head instead of assuming it. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="master_closure_linear_ring.wav"
                         anchorOnset="1" startFrame="137320" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.0796" maxRms="0.0845"/>

    <!-- ===== THE DISPLACEMENT, INJECTED =============================
         The same captured audio, dumped again with leading silence in front
         of it (QBX-222's leadSilenceFrames, which exists for exactly this).
         The capture is cumulative and playback has stopped, so every file
         below carries identical audio and differs only in where its head sits.

         This is what makes the anchor a gate rather than a hope: it
         reproduces CI's failure LOCALLY and DETERMINISTICALLY. Measured:

           absolute window, head displaced 2048 frames    0.0845219
           CI's failing value, twice, on two commits      0.0845199
           anchored window, same displaced file           0.0820714
           anchored window, clean file                    0.0820714

         The first two agree to the resolution of the measurement; the last
         two are bit-identical, because the onset moved 30680 to 32728 -
         exactly the 2048 injected - and the window followed.

         TWO INJECTIONS, AND NEITHER SIZE IS ARBITRARY.

         4096 is the one with a usable RMS MARGIN. The material plateaus below
         the window, so displacement does not buy proportionally more level: at
         2048 the absolute window reads +2.99 % and at 48000 only +3.02 %, both
         of them a hair over the 0.0845 ceiling. At 4096 it reads 0.0868246,
         +5.79 %, which is out of band by a real distance rather than by a
         rounding digit.

         48000 is the one that proves the INJECTION HAPPENED, independently of
         any band. The clean capture's onset is bounded above by 40000 frames
         six lines up, so an onset at or past 40000 in the padded file can only
         have come from the injection - whatever mode the box is in. That
         matters because the box has its own 2048-frame modes: a displacement
         that small can be mimicked by one, so an expectReject over a 2048
         injection alone could be satisfied by a run that injected nothing.
         Measured, exactly that way round: a sabotage disabling the injection
         left this case GREEN because the box happened to be in the displaced
         mode that run.
         ================================================================= -->
    <dump-playback-capture filename="mclr_head_4096.wav"
                           minFrames="216000" leadSilenceFrames="4096"/>

    <!-- The anchor absorbs it: same band, same audio, head moved. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mclr_head_4096.wav"
                         anchorOnset="1" startFrame="137320" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.0796" maxRms="0.0845"/>

    <!-- ...and the ABSOLUTE window does not, by 5.79 %. Without this line the
         case could not tell an anchor that works from a displacement that
         never happened. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mclr_head_4096.wav"
                         startFrame="168000" frameCount="48000" channel="0"
                         minRms="0.0796" maxRms="0.0845" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- A FAR larger displacement, to pin that the injection is real and that
         the anchor tracks an arbitrary one rather than a tuned one. -->
    <dump-playback-capture filename="mclr_head_48000.wav"
                           minFrames="216000" leadSilenceFrames="48000"/>
    <assert-audio-onset filename="mclr_head_48000.wav" channel="0"
                        minFrame="40000"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mclr_head_48000.wav"
                         anchorOnset="1" startFrame="137320" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.0796" maxRms="0.0845"/>

    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="0"/>

    <!-- AC3.4: nobody rendered where they were not allowed to. Under Closure
         the pump runs the master lane's OWN processor instances, so a freeze
         worker touching one of them is exactly the two-thread hazard D4b
         names. -->
    <assert-render-policy liveThreadRefusals="0" liveOwnedRefusals="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="2"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
